      "originalText": "Excellent questions. These are exactly the points where many engineers get confused when \nlearning event-driven systems.\n\n---\n\n# First Understand Why We Need Workers\n\nSuppose Razorpay sends:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"event_id\":\"evt_102\",\n  \"event_type\":\"payment.captured\",\n  \"payment_id\":\"pay_1\"\n}\n```\n\nThe webhook API receives it.\n\nNow what work needs to happen?\n\n```text\n1. Verify Signature\n2. Save Event\n3. Update Payment State\n4. Update Ledger\n5. Send Notification\n6. Trigger Settlement\n7. Write Audit Log\n```\n\nIf Webhook API does all of this:\n\n```text\nRazorpay\n   |\n   v\nWebhook API\n   |\n   +--> Update Payment\n   +--> Update Ledger\n   +--> Send Email\n   +--> Send SMS\n   +--> Trigger Settlement\n```\n\nmaybe it takes:\n\n```text\n4-5 seconds\n```\n\nRequirement says:\n\n```text\nMust ACK within 2 seconds\n```\n\nSo we move heavy work to workers.\n\n---\n\n# What Does Webhook API Actually Do?\n\nVery little.\n\n```text\nReceive Request\n    ↓\nVerify HMAC\n    ↓\nVerify Timestamp\n    ↓\nStore Event\n    ↓\nPush To Kafka\n    ↓\nReturn 200 OK\n```\n\nDone.\n\n---\n\n# Does Worker Consume Kafka?\n\n**Yes.**\n\nThat is usually the purpose of a worker.\n\nThink:\n\n```text\nProducer --> Kafka --> Consumer\n```\n\nHere:\n\n```text\nWebhook API = Producer\n\nKafka = Queue\n\nWorker = Consumer\n```\n\n---\n\nVisualize:\n\n```text\n              Kafka\n\n         evt_101\n         evt_102\n         evt_103\n         evt_104\n\n              |\n              |\n              v\n\n       Payment Worker\n```\n\nWorker continuously does:\n\n```python\nwhile True:\n    event = kafka.consume()\n    process(event)\n```\n\nConceptually.\n\n---\n\n# How Many Workers Do We Need?\n\nLet's see the tasks.\n\n---\n\n## Worker Type 1: Payment Processor Worker\n\nConsumes webhook events.\n\nResponsible for:\n\n```text\n1. Read Event\n2. Validate State Transition\n3. Update payments table\n4. Insert state history\n5. Create outbox event\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```text\npayment.authorized\npayment.captured\npayment.settled\n```\n\nall processed here.\n\n---\n\nArchitecture:\n\n```text\nKafka\n   |\n   v\nPayment Worker\n```\n\n---\n\n# Why Not Send Notification Directly Here?\n\nBecause notification may fail.\n\nEmail service may be down.\n\nSMS service may be down.\n\nWe don't want payment processing blocked.\n\nSo we separate concerns.\n\n---\n\n## Worker Type 2: Notification Worker\n\nResponsible for:\n\n```text\ncaptured\n```\n\nevents.\n\nReads:\n\n```text\noutbox_events\n```\n\nor another Kafka topic.\n\nThen:\n\n```text\nSend Email\nSend Push Notification\nSend SMS\n```\n\n---\n\nArchitecture:\n\n```text\noutbox_events\n      |\n      v\nNotification Worker\n```\n\n---\n\n## Worker Type 3: Reconciliation Worker\n\nResponsible for:\n\n```text\nsettled\n```\n\nevents.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nMerchant received money\n```\n\nNeed reconciliation.\n\nThis can be slow.\n\nSeparate worker.\n\n---\n\nArchitecture:\n\n```text\noutbox_events\n      |\n      v\nReconciliation Worker\n```\n\n---\n\n# So Total Workers?\n\nFor interview:\n\n```text\n1. Payment Processor Worker\n\n2. Notification Worker\n\n3. Reconciliation Worker\n```\n\nis a very good answer.\n\n---\n\n# Complete Flow\n\nLet's walk through one event.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1\n\nRazorpay sends:\n\n```text\npayment.captured\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 2\n\nWebhook API\n\n```text\nVerify Signature\nVerify Timestamp\nInsert webhook_events\nPublish Kafka\nACK\n```\n\n---\n\nKafka now contains:\n\n```text\nevt_102\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 3\n\nPayment Worker consumes Kafka\n\n```text\nKafka\n   |\n   v\nPayment Worker\n```\n\nWorker:\n\n```text\nCurrent State = authorized\n\nIncoming = captured\n\nValid?\nYes\n```\n\nUpdate:\n\n```text\nauthorized\n    ↓\ncaptured\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 4\n\nPayment Worker inserts:\n\n```text\noutbox_events\n\npay_1\ncaptured\nPENDING\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 5\n\nNotification Worker sees:\n\n```text\ncaptured\n```\n\nNeeds notification.\n\nSends:\n\n```text\nEmail\nSMS\nPush Notification\n```\n\nMarks outbox:\n\n```text\nCOMPLETED\n```\n\n---\n\n# Why Separate Workers?\n\nImagine notification service is down.\n\nWithout separation:\n\n```text\nPayment Update\n     ↓\nNotification Fails\n     ↓\nEntire processing fails\n```\n\nBad.\n\n---\n\nWith separation:\n\n```text\nPayment Update Success\n     ↓\nOutbox Created\n     ↓\nNotification Worker Retry Later\n```\n\nPayment state remains correct.\n\n---\n\n# Real Production Scaling\n\nAt 500 webhooks/sec:\n\n```text\nKafka\n   |\n   +---- Payment Worker 1\n   +---- Payment Worker 2\n   +---- Payment Worker 3\n   +---- Payment Worker 4\n```\n\nAll consume in parallel.\n\n---\n\nSimilarly:\n\n```text\nNotification Queue\n\n   |\n   +---- Notification Worker 1\n   +---- Notification Worker 2\n   +---- Notification Worker 3\n```\n\n---\n\n# Interview Answer\n\nIf interviewer asks:\n\n> Does Worker consume Kafka?\n\nAnswer:\n\n```text\nYes. Webhook API publishes events to Kafka.\nPayment Processor Workers consume those events,\nupdate payment state, and create outbox entries.\nSeparate Notification and Reconciliation workers\nprocess the outbox to execute side effects.\n```\n\nThink of it as:\n\n```text\nWebhook API = Receptionist\n\nKafka = Waiting Room\n\nWorkers = Employees doing actual work\n```\n\nThat mental model makes most event-driven architectures much easier to visualize.\n",
