      "updated": 1780628399176,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Howmany requests or events can kafka store",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Howmany requests or events can kafka store",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Z5TtYxDaWamLobGshgNRz",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -273.7822540717759,
      "y": 25716.639821731584,
      "width": 3333,
      "height": 7050,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b08",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 845833258,
      "version": 29,
      "versionNonce": 2076284086,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1780628397677,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The short answer is:\n\n> **Kafka does not have a fixed limit on the number of events it can store.**\n>\n> It can store **millions, billions, or even trillions of events**, depending on disk space, \nretention settings, and cluster size.\n\nLet's build intuition.\n\n---\n\n# Think of Kafka as a Huge Log File\n\nImagine a file:\n\n```text\nEvent 1\nEvent 2\nEvent 3\nEvent 4\n...\nEvent 1000000\n```\n\nKafka keeps appending new events to the end.\n\n```text\n┌─────────────────┐\n│ Event 1         │\n│ Event 2         │\n│ Event 3         │\n│ Event 4         │\n│ ...             │\n└─────────────────┘\n```\n\nUnlike a queue that removes messages after consumption, Kafka keeps them for a configured period.\n\n---\n\n# What Limits Kafka Storage?\n\nMainly:\n\n### 1. Disk Space\n\nSuppose:\n\n```text\nEach event = 1 KB\n1 million events = ~1 GB\n1 billion events = ~1 TB\n```\n\nIf your Kafka brokers have:\n\n```text\n10 TB disk\n```\n\nyou can store roughly:\n\n```text\n10 billion 1-KB events\n```\n---\n\n### 2. Retention Policy\n\nKafka doesn't keep data forever unless you tell it to.\nExample:\n```properties\nretention.ms=604800000\n```\n\nmeans:\n```text\nKeep data for 7 days\n```\n\nAfter 7 days Kafka deletes old segments.\n---\n\nAnother example:\n```properties\nretention.bytes=100GB\n```\n\nmeans:\n```text\nKeep only the latest 100 GB\n```\n\nOlder data is removed.\n---\n# For Your Webhook System\n\nAverage:\n```text\n50 events/sec\n```\n\nBurst:\n```text\n500 events/sec\n```\n\nLet's calculate.\n---\n\n## Average Traffic\n\n```text\n50 events/sec\n```\n\nPer day:\n\n```text\n50 × 60 × 60 × 24\n\n= 4,320,000 events/day\n```\n\nAbout:\n\n```text\n4.3 million events/day\n```\n---\n\nSuppose each webhook event is:\n\n```text\n2 KB\n```\n\nStorage per day:\n\n```text\n4.3 million × 2 KB\n\n≈ 8.6 GB/day\n```\n---\n\n7-day retention:\n\n```text\n8.6 × 7\n\n≈ 60 GB\n```\n\nVery manageable.\n\n---\n\n# What Happens If Workers Are Down?\n\nThis is where Kafka shines.\n\nSuppose:\n\n```text\nWebhook API\n    ↓\nKafka\n```\n\nis receiving:\n\n```text\n500 events/sec\n```\n\nbut workers crash.\n\n---\n\nKafka keeps storing:\n\n```text\nevt_1\nevt_2\nevt_3\nevt_4\n...\nevt_100000\n```\n\non disk.\n\nNothing is lost.\n\n---\n\nWorkers come back later:\n\n```text\nKafka\n   ↓\nWorker\n```\n\nand continue reading from where they stopped.\n\nThis is called:\n\n```text\nDurable Message Storage\n```\n\n---\n\n# How Much Throughput Can Kafka Handle?\n\nA single broker can often handle:\n\n```text\n100,000+\nmessages/sec\n```\n\ndepending on:\n\n* message size\n* hardware\n* replication factor\n\nA Kafka cluster can handle:\n\n```text\nMillions of messages/sec\n```\n\nwith enough brokers and partitions.\n\n---\n\n# In Your Interview Design\n\nFor:\n\n```text\n50 req/sec average\n500 req/sec burst\n```\n\nKafka is actually overkill.\n\nIt can comfortably absorb:\n\n```text\n500/sec\n```\n\nwithout any issue.\n\nEven:\n\n```text\n5,000/sec\n50,000/sec\n```\n\nwould still be realistic with a properly configured cluster.\n\n---\n\n# A Useful Interview Follow-up\n\nIf an interviewer asks:\n\n> Why Kafka instead of RabbitMQ/SQS?\n\nYou can say:\n\n> Kafka stores events durably on disk and allows consumers to replay events. If workers are down for several hours, Kafka can retain the backlog and workers can resume from their last committed offset. This makes it well-suited for webhook ingestion systems where reliability and recovery are important.\n\nThat's usually the answer they're looking for.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The short answer is:\n\n> **Kafka does not have a fixed limit on the number of events it can store.**\n>\n> It can store **millions, billions, or even trillions of events**, depending on disk space, \nretention settings, and cluster size.\n\nLet's build intuition.\n\n---\n\n# Think of Kafka as a Huge Log File\n\nImagine a file:\n\n```text\nEvent 1\nEvent 2\nEvent 3\nEvent 4\n...\nEvent 1000000\n```\n\nKafka keeps appending new events to the end.\n\n```text\n┌─────────────────┐\n│ Event 1         │\n│ Event 2         │\n│ Event 3         │\n│ Event 4         │\n│ ...             │\n└─────────────────┘\n```\n\nUnlike a queue that removes messages after consumption, Kafka keeps them for a configured period.\n\n---\n\n# What Limits Kafka Storage?\n\nMainly:\n\n### 1. Disk Space\n\nSuppose:\n\n```text\nEach event = 1 KB\n1 million events = ~1 GB\n1 billion events = ~1 TB\n```\n\nIf your Kafka brokers have:\n\n```text\n10 TB disk\n```\n\nyou can store roughly:\n\n```text\n10 billion 1-KB events\n```\n---\n\n### 2. Retention Policy\n\nKafka doesn't keep data forever unless you tell it to.\nExample:\n```properties\nretention.ms=604800000\n```\n\nmeans:\n```text\nKeep data for 7 days\n```\n\nAfter 7 days Kafka deletes old segments.\n---\n\nAnother example:\n```properties\nretention.bytes=100GB\n```\n\nmeans:\n```text\nKeep only the latest 100 GB\n```\n\nOlder data is removed.\n---\n# For Your Webhook System\n\nAverage:\n```text\n50 events/sec\n```\n\nBurst:\n```text\n500 events/sec\n```\n\nLet's calculate.\n---\n\n## Average Traffic\n\n```text\n50 events/sec\n```\n\nPer day:\n\n```text\n50 × 60 × 60 × 24\n\n= 4,320,000 events/day\n```\n\nAbout:\n\n```text\n4.3 million events/day\n```\n---\n\nSuppose each webhook event is:\n\n```text\n2 KB\n```\n\nStorage per day:\n\n```text\n4.3 million × 2 KB\n\n≈ 8.6 GB/day\n```\n---\n\n7-day retention:\n\n```text\n8.6 × 7\n\n≈ 60 GB\n```\n\nVery manageable.\n\n---\n\n# What Happens If Workers Are Down?\n\nThis is where Kafka shines.\n\nSuppose:\n\n```text\nWebhook API\n    ↓\nKafka\n```\n\nis receiving:\n\n```text\n500 events/sec\n```\n\nbut workers crash.\n\n---\n\nKafka keeps storing:\n\n```text\nevt_1\nevt_2\nevt_3\nevt_4\n...\nevt_100000\n```\n\non disk.\n\nNothing is lost.\n\n---\n\nWorkers come back later:\n\n```text\nKafka\n   ↓\nWorker\n```\n\nand continue reading from where they stopped.\n\nThis is called:\n\n```text\nDurable Message Storage\n```\n\n---\n\n# How Much Throughput Can Kafka Handle?\n\nA single broker can often handle:\n\n```text\n100,000+\nmessages/sec\n```\n\ndepending on:\n\n* message size\n* hardware\n* replication factor\n\nA Kafka cluster can handle:\n\n```text\nMillions of messages/sec\n```\n\nwith enough brokers and partitions.\n\n---\n\n# In Your Interview Design\n\nFor:\n\n```text\n50 req/sec average\n500 req/sec burst\n```\n\nKafka is actually overkill.\n\nIt can comfortably absorb:\n\n```text\n500/sec\n```\n\nwithout any issue.\n\nEven:\n\n```text\n5,000/sec\n50,000/sec\n```\n\nwould still be realistic with a properly configured cluster.\n\n---\n\n# A Useful Interview Follow-up\n\nIf an interviewer asks:\n\n> Why Kafka instead of RabbitMQ/SQS?\n\nYou can say:\n\n> Kafka stores events durably on disk and allows consumers to replay events. If workers are down for several hours, Kafka can retain the backlog and workers can resume from their last committed offset. This makes it well-suited for webhook ingestion systems where reliability and recovery are important.\n\nThat's usually the answer they're looking for.\n",
